{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791070321,"nonce":"+zskTC59aLbxWljbbAjiOyaUDPA=","title":"Cascade: a class, id or attribute change re-matched only that element; children-change hooks ran before the change and missed fragments","message":"1. Identity hooks marked only the changed element, but .a \u003e .b, .a .b, .a + .b and .a ~ .b depend on it, so the elements those reach kept stale styles. Seen as list bullets that never showed: li \u003e .marker.disc \u003e .bullet, with .disc set after the bullet was first styled.\n\n2. LayoutTree.childrenHooks run before each change, while the children are the old ones, so work done in the hook (list numbering, table columns, fieldset disabling) saw the old children. And a change under a fragment (a \u003cfor\u003e) named only the fragment, not the node its items are laid out in, so an \u003col\u003e did not renumber when a \u003cfor\u003e inside it added an item.\n\nFix: markChanged marks the element's subtree and its later siblings' subtrees. childrenChanged also notifies the node a fragment is laid out in. ashui.layout.AfterChildren runs registered work at the start of the next flush, after the changes and before the stylesheet restyles.","files":null}]}